e it's  measuring the raw power of the graphics  card so that's very good news for the  base model since by itself it scored  less than 7000 points we  has said units heaven gaming benchmark  and we got basically the same results  with both MacBooks but since those are  just benchmarks let's get into real  world gaming performance first we  started off with the relatively easy to  run league of legends with in Mac OS and  both setups were able to get an average  of around 140 FPS at 4k resolution with  all the settings maxed out so this game  was a cakewalk with no real difference  between both of these however we then  switched over to fortnight and this is  where we started to see a difference we  played at 1080p resolution at high  settings on both machines and the base  model with the GPU got an average of  around 65 FPS with the minimum fps  dipping down to 46 at times overall it  wasn't a smoother man experience as I'd  hoped for but it should get better if  you turn down some of the settings now  on the $1,800 MacBook Pro with the each  of you I noticed that gameplay was more  smooth in general with less frame drops  in dips than the base model with this  setup I noticed a much better 85 FPS  average with frame rates rarely ever  dipping below 60 fps so this gave in  actually great gaming experience on  these settings since you're at a minimum  of 60 fps almost all the time and one  thing I did notice is that on the base  model the fans were a little bit louder  as well now let's get into video editing  with Final Cut Pro 10 in the Bruce X  test we can see that the e GPU greatly  helped out the base macbook pro since it  took a very long 51 seconds by itself  but the $1,800 model would the a GPU  still beat it out by two seconds which  isn't that much however in our twenty  second 4k stabilization test the a GPU  was much slower with the base model than  with the $1,800 model in fact the  higher-end model by itself took only  four seconds longer than the base model  with the e GPU which is very impressive  and we honestly don't know what's  limiting the base model that much now  the first major discovery that we made  was during our five minute  que h.264 export test which is the codec  that most youtubers use the base model  with the e GPU was not only twice as  slow as the $1,800 model with a GPU but  it was even slower than that model by  itself which was totally unexpected one  thing we noticed during the export test  was that the fans on the base model were  much louder blowing at around 5,800 rpm  compared to a much quieter 4000 rpm on  the $1,800 model while connected to the  e GPU now we think there are two reasons  for this the $1,800 model actually comes  with dual fans compared to just one on  the base model so that's a pretty good  advantage but the even bigger deal is  that the $1,800 model comes with two  extra vents on the bottom side so when  you use your MacBook and clamshell mode  like most people do while using an e GPU  the fans can easily draw cool air from  those vents now since the base model  doesn't get those vents it doesn't get  cool air as efficiently so the single  fan has to work much harder to match the  cooling performance of the $1,800 models  dual fans and it ultimately runs louder  now back to that unexpected result in  the export test there's a chance that  the main limitation is actually the  eight gigabytes of Base RAM on that base  model but unfortunately we can't confirm  this right now because we don't have the  base model with the 16 gigabyte RAM  upgrade moving on to the five-minute 4kh  EVC export test we again saw the same  results with the $1,800 model by itself  being faster than the base model with  the e GPU which is just insane we took a  screenshot during this test to prove  that we have the 5700 XT graphics card  selected and this screenshot also shows  that for some weird reason the  integrated graphics on the base model is  being favored instead of the e GPU which  was weird because that didn't happen on  the higher-end  $1800 model the only reasonable  explanation is at the tension ship on  that higher-end model features new  technology that somehow improves the  efficiency of using EGP use like for  example  the PCH is now on package which  basically means it's integrated into the  CPU so maybe because of that the EGP you  can more quickly and easily communicate  with the processor and therefore boost a  GPU performance compared to the old 8th  gen champ now for our final video  editing test exporting a c200 raw 60fps  clip which has a much tougher codec to  work with the $1,800.00 pro with the a  GPU once again performed the best so  after running all of those tests we  figured out the best value options for  each different use case if you mainly  want to get some better gaming  performance the base model with an e GPU  should be fine for you but the $1,800  model did perform quite a bit better for  fortnight so if you can afford that set  up it's the best way to go since it'll  perform better than the 16 inch macbook  pro by itself now for 3d rendering or  other apps that mainly only use graphics  power  the base model should be just fine but  we strongly recommend spending the extra  $100 on the 16 gigabyte RAM upgrade and  finally for video editing we actually  recommend buying the $1,800 MacBook Pro  by itself without an e GPU because of  how well it performed beating out the  base model with the e GPU in a couple  different tests and later on if you feel  like it's still not fast enough for your  use case you can then invest into an e  GP you set up if you really want to but  keep in mind our results from our last  video we saw that the 16 inch MacBook  Pro by itself actually outperform that  $1,800 model combined with the e GPU  quite a few different times so if you  don't mind the larger size of the 16  inch MacBook Pro and you have the cash  to spend on it right now that is  actually the best choice for video  editing now I'm not trying to say that  the base model with the e GPU is bad for  video editing since it does a pretty  good job for a lot of the common 4k  editing tasks but it just doesn't  compare to the $1,800 model for those  who can't afford it so if this video  helps you make a decision on which one  to buy you can find the links to the Mac  books  in the EGP you parts down in the  description below and go ahead and tap  that like button and click the circle  above so you don't miss out on more  videos like this one and make sure to  check out those each of you videos right  over there those are the ones that I  mentioned earlier in the video thanks  for watching and I'll see you in the  next one  [Music]
 

